You need the March scanner receipts, not the February ones, and
there are more names in the directory than you want to type.
You could pick the files one by one, or you could describe the
part of their names that matters. Before you copy anything, though,
you want to see which names that description actually picks. A
filename pattern is good at making a selection; checking the
selection is still your job.

\begin{objectives}
\item Predict which pathnames match a glob with wildcards or brackets.
\item Explain why a filename beginning with a dot needs special treatment.
\item Preview matches and distinguish shell globs from utility patterns.
\end{objectives}

\section{Make a small set you can predict}
\label{sec:14-practice}
Use \cmd|sh| for the worked examples so your interactive shell's
settings don't accidentally change what you're learning. Type
\cmd|sh| at your ordinary prompt, then run the setup at the new
prompt. Exit this practice shell with \cmd|exit| after cleanup.

\begin{terminal}
lab=$(mktemp -d) && cd "$lab" && pwd
\end{terminal}

Stop if the setup does not print the path of a new directory. Note
that path; \cmd|cd| changes the working directory only if
\cmd|mktemp -d| succeeds. Here \cmd|$(...)| captures the printed name
and the quoted \cmd|"$lab"| passes it as one argument. Work only in
the directory you just created. \cmd|touch| creates each named file
if it doesn't exist; on an existing file it updates its timestamps.
The \cmd|mkdir| call creates a separate directory for an additional
receipt. No personal receipt is needed.

\begin{terminal}
touch march-1.txt march-2.txt march-12.txt 'march coffee.txt'
touch feb-1.txt march-1.pdf .march-3.txt
mkdir archive
touch archive/march-4.txt
\end{terminal}

The files are empty. Their names, not their contents, are the
subject of these examples. The archive receipt is one level deeper
and the hidden one begins with a dot. The March coffee filename
includes a space; the quotes around the name in the setup make it
one argument rather than two. Keep the file set unchanged until
you have finished comparing the patterns.

\section{Let the shell list its choices}
\label{sec:14-expand}
\IndexTerm{glob}
\IndexTerm{pathname expansion}
\IndexTerm{wildcard}
An unquoted pattern like \cmd|./march*.txt| is a \emph{glob}:
the shell looks at names relative to the working directory and
replaces the pattern with the matching pathnames, each as a separate
argument. The \cmd|*| can match any sequence of characters, including
an empty sequence, subject to the pathname rules below. The other
characters in this pattern have to match literally. The dot before
\texttt{txt} is a dot, not a wildcard. The \cmd|./| makes each
resulting path start with a dot rather than with a possible option
hyphen, as discussed in Chapter~\ref{ch:13}.

\begin{terminal}
printf '[%s]\n' ./march*.txt
\end{terminal}

Expect four lines naming \texttt{march coffee.txt} and the three
March text receipts numbered 1, 2, and 12. Compare membership,
not order: filename order depends on the shell's locale and
sorting behavior. The \cmd|printf| format surrounds each argument
with brackets and adds a newline. The space in the coffee name
stays inside one argument. Pathname expansion supplies whole paths;
it doesn't split up each path again at spaces. Quoting the whole
pattern would keep the wildcard itself literal instead.

\begin{terminal}
printf '[%s]\n' './march*.txt'
\end{terminal}

This time the one line is \texttt{[./march*.txt]}. It prints that
literal string even though there is no file with that name. A
\cmd|printf| preview displays arguments, not proof that every
argument names a file. That distinction becomes crucial when a
pattern finds nothing. Figure~\ref{fig:14-stages} separates the
reader's spelling, the shell's selection, and the displayed list.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering\input{figures/chapter-14/stages.tikz}
\caption{The receiving command prints the selected paths, not the pattern.}
\label{fig:14-stages}
\VisualDescription{An unquoted glob enters the shell; one separate argument leaves for each match. The receiving command sees the paths without knowing how they were found.}
\end{figure}

This order of events is why \cmd|ls| and \cmd|printf| agree about
which filenames the shell gave them even if they display them
differently. It also explains why enclosing the expansion in a
single pair of quotes is not a general fix for spaces. Here we want
many arguments, potentially including names with spaces, not one
argument containing a wildcard. In Chapter~\ref{ch:25} you'll see
how to quote just the literal pieces of a more complicated pattern.

\section{Choose the number of characters}
\label{sec:14-shapes}
The question mark matches exactly one character, not ``any receipt
number.'' That statement doesn't require you to know the alphabet
of the filename: a letter or punctuation mark could match too.
To express a limited list of possibilities for one position, put
the possibilities inside brackets. Using explicit digits avoids
subtle assumptions about how a locale treats ranges.

\begin{terminal}
printf '[%s]\n' ./march-?.txt
printf '[%s]\n' ./march-[12].txt
printf '[%s]\n' ./march-[!2].txt
\end{terminal}

The first line of input finds the files numbered 1 and 2 but not
12; neither bracket expression can match the two-character
number 12 either. The second line accepts either the character
\texttt{1} or the character \texttt{2}; it produces the same
selection here but wouldn't select \texttt{march-A.txt}. The
exclamation mark immediately after the opening bracket in the third
pattern means ``anything allowed in this position except
\texttt{2}.'' In the fixture that matches only the file numbered
1. A bracket expression is one choice at one position; it is not
an instruction to match the bracket's entire contents in sequence.

\begin{quicktable}{M{1.6in}Y}
\toprule
Pattern & Reading it as a pathname glob \\
\midrule
./march*.txt & Literal prefix and suffix with any sequence between. \\
./march-?.txt & Exactly one character between the hyphen and the suffix. \\
./march-[12].txt & Exactly one of the characters 1 and 2 at that position. \\
./march-[!2].txt & Exactly one character there, other than 2. \\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

Bracket ranges such as \cmd|[a-z]| are common, but which characters
fall in a range can depend on locale and implementation. For a
portable task with a small selection, list the characters you
actually want. If the pattern is meant to encode a naming scheme,
start by testing its borderline cases. Would \texttt{march-01.txt}
count? What about \texttt{March-1.txt}? A wildcard cannot decide
whether you consider these equivalent. Case sensitivity and the
meaning of a single character are separate questions; do not infer
either merely from the way the name looks on your screen.

\section{Respect the slash and the first dot}
\label{sec:14-components}
\IndexTerm{hidden file}
For pathname expansion, a wildcard does not eat the slash that
separates directory components. Nor does it match a leading dot in
a component under normal \cmd|sh| rules. To see the hidden receipt
you can ask for a pattern that explicitly begins the final component
with a dot. To see the archive receipt, you can specify the
directory as a separate component of the path.

\begin{terminal}
printf '[%s]\n' ./.march-?.txt
printf '[%s]\n' ./archive/march-?.txt
\end{terminal}

Expect \texttt{./.march-3.txt} and \texttt{./archive/march-4.txt},
respectively, each inside brackets. The leading dot rule applies
to each component, not just to the beginning of the full path.
The first dot of the \cmd|./| prefix names the current directory;
it doesn't count as explicitly matching the dot of the last
component. Some shells have optional settings that alter hidden
matching or give a doubled star a recursive meaning. Neither is
needed to reason about the examples here.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering\input{figures/chapter-14/components.tikz}
\caption{Crossing into a directory takes an explicit slash in the pattern.}
\label{fig:14-components}
\VisualDescription{The path is divided at the slashes into a current-directory marker, an archive component, and a filename pattern. The last wildcard operates only in the filename component.}
\end{figure}

\begin{terminal}
find . -type f -name 'march-?.txt' -print
\end{terminal}

This is the find-by-name operation from Chapter~\ref{ch:9}.
\cmd|.| tells \cmd|find| where to start, \cmd|-type f| chooses
regular files, \cmd|-name| tests each individual final component,
and \cmd|-print| prints matching paths. Here the quotes are
essential: they send the pattern intact for \cmd|find| to interpret,
instead of asking the shell for names before \cmd|find| starts.
Expect the files numbered 1, 2, and 4. The hidden receipt isn't a
match because its name begins with a literal dot but the pattern
begins with \texttt{march}. The archive match comes from
\cmd|find| descending into directories, not from a wildcard
consuming a slash. \cmd|find -name| and the shell's pathname
expansion have distinct rules; read the correct manual.

\section{No match is also a result}
\label{sec:14-no-match}
Try an April pattern while there are still no April files:

\begin{terminal}
printf '[%s]\n' ./april*.txt
\end{terminal}

Under normal POSIX \cmd|sh| behavior, the result is the original
pattern inside brackets, not an empty list and not an April file.
In default Bash the behavior is similar; \cmd|nullglob| can
remove the unmatched argument and \cmd|failglob| can cause an
expansion error. Zsh normally reports an error for an unmatched
pattern and doesn't run the command. Fish normally also rejects
unmatched wildcards for ordinary commands, with exceptions for
some builtins. Shell settings and modes matter; a shell name
alone is not enough to describe a customized session.

\begin{quicktable}{M{1.35in}Y}
\toprule
Situation & What the preview is telling you \\
\midrule
One or more matches & The shell gives each selected path its own argument. Verify the set, and check file types if that matters. \\
No match in POSIX sh & The unchanged pattern is passed as an argument; ordinary text printed by a utility need not name an existing file. \\
No match in default Zsh & The shell usually rejects the command before the utility runs. \\
Pattern quoted for find & The shell passes the pattern as written; find evaluates it as it visits each entry. \\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

A matching path can name a directory as well as a regular file;
the glob knows only the name. Preview again if you move to a
different directory or if files have arrived since your original
preview. A glob is not a frozen selection you can carry between
commands. For a small, high-stakes action, explicitly naming the
few confirmed files is sensible. Never trade away the preview
just to save typing. These examples use harmless names; unless
you know that your real filenames don't contain newlines, one
line of output is not even an unambiguous representation of one
argument. The actual argument boundaries still exist, whether
your display makes them easy to see or not.

\section{The same star can ask a different question}
\label{sec:14-languages}
\IndexTerm{regular expression}
Chapter~\ref{ch:19} will introduce the regular expressions
used to search text inside files. Don't borrow the meaning of a
glob's star when reading a regular expression. In a glob, the
star is itself a wildcard for a sequence. In the regular
expression \cmd|mar*| the star means zero or more copies of
the preceding \texttt{r}; the expression could match
\texttt{ma}, \texttt{mar}, or \texttt{marrr}, depending on the
surrounding text. A shell pathname glob and a text-search utility
also look in different places, so even identical-looking
patterns would not have the same job. Ask ``which thing reads
this notation?'' before asking ``what does it match?''

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering\input{figures/chapter-14/languages.tikz}
\caption{One pattern can be interpreted in two different places.}
\label{fig:14-languages}
\VisualDescription{An unquoted path pattern is expanded by the shell first. A quoted pattern reaches find untouched, and find interprets it for each visited name.}
\end{figure}

\begin{dialoguebox}{Who reads the pattern?}
\Brian{You said the quotes stop a pattern. Why does the quoted pattern still work with \texttt{find}?}
\Stephen{The quotes stop the shell interpreting it.}
\Brian{Then does \texttt{find} receive the quotes as instructions?}
\Stephen{No. It receives the pattern itself. Its own \texttt{-name} test interprets that pattern.}
\Brian{So if I omit the quotes, \texttt{find} might receive two filenames?}
\Stephen{Yes. With two matches it can receive two arguments where it expected one pattern.}
\Brian{That is not a different search; I changed the question before it arrived.}
\Stephen{Exactly. Keep the pattern intact for the command that should read it.}
\DialogueConclusion{Name the interpreter before choosing the quotes.}
\end{dialoguebox}

\section{Plan a selection before using it}
\label{sec:14-plan}
Suppose the organizer wants all numbered March text receipts.
On the original lab, \cmd|./march-*.txt| makes that selection.
Notice how little this pattern says: it matches the literal
hyphen and suffix with any characters between. If a file
arrives named \texttt{march-summary.txt}, it fits the pattern
even though no date appears in the name. You can see the
problem without involving real files by adding a dummy name
just long enough to try two selections.

\begin{terminal}
touch march-summary.txt
printf '[%s]\n' ./march-*.txt
printf '[%s]\n' ./march-[12].txt
\end{terminal}

The broad request now includes the summary; the narrow
request doesn't, but it also leaves out number 12. A pattern
that seems perfect for a fixed handful of names can be a poor
contract for a directory that changes every day. For real
receipts, decide which case matters: a short selection you
can read individually, or a long collection that conforms
to a verified naming rule. An unexpected name should prompt
you to question the rule, not to accept the broadest
wildcard. The final cleanup also names the dummy summary.

In a shell set to remove unmatched patterns, the shell may
pass \cmd|printf| the format alone. A \cmd|%s| without a
supplied value is replaced by an empty string, so your
screen might show \cmd|[]| rather than showing no lines.
That is not an empty filename in the directory. In the
default \cmd|sh| exercise the pattern doesn't disappear,
and you see the pattern inside the same brackets. The
receiving utility matters even in a read-only preview.

You don't have to do everything with a single glob. If you
want the March text receipts both here and one level down,
write two paths or patterns. If you want to cover
descendants however deep, use \cmd|find| and quote the
name rule. A glob like \cmd|./*/march-?.txt| describes
precisely one directory component between the working
directory and the filename. It cannot match a receipt
already in the working directory or one two levels down.

\begin{terminal}
printf '[%s]\n' ./march-?.txt ./*/march-?.txt
\end{terminal}

In the lab each pattern finds a match. Expect the numbered
1 and 2 files from the first word, then the number 4 receipt
from the second. In a new directory, one pattern could find
matches while the other remains literal in \cmd|sh|. That
is a reason to read the entire preview rather than stopping
at the first name that looks right. It is also a reason
not to substitute \cmd|ls|'s formatting for argument
boundaries: the listing may place several names on one
line, but the argument list still has one entry per match.

Another way to check your rule is to invent one example to
include and one near miss to exclude. The literal hyphen
excludes the coffee filename; two bracket expressions can
accept exactly two digits; an explicit leading dot can
include the hidden receipt. None proves a file is a real
receipt, or that its date belongs to March. Derive the
selection from what you know about names, and confirm any
other property separately before using the files.

A usable naming scheme makes these checks less surprising.
Think about receipts named after dates, such as
\texttt{2026-03-01.txt}. You can select a month by its literal
prefix without guessing whether \texttt{March},
\texttt{march}, or \texttt{Mar} was used. Fixed-width day
numbers also spare you the difference between one character
and two. Renaming existing files to impose a scheme is a
separate task and might affect links or shared references;
don't try it just to prove a glob works. Test a proposed
scheme on new sample names first. For this exercise, use
the actual filenames and treat their inconsistent lengths
as data, not as a mistake the shell should correct.

Some extensions that look like pathname globs aren't portable
glob syntax at all. For instance, several shells can generate
alternatives from braces, but POSIX \cmd|sh| does not specify
that behavior. The literal slash, question mark, star, and
bracket forms here suffice for these tasks. Don't rewrite an
example with a more elaborate spelling until you know which
shell will interpret it. If you're helping someone who uses
a different shell, share the exact command and expected
matches rather than telling them only that the wildcard works.

\section{Practice and cleanup}
\begin{exercise}{Take just the two-digit receipt}
Preview the March text receipts with exactly two digits after
the hyphen. Use an explicit list of digits twice, not a range.
Predict the one selected argument first.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
Use \cmd|printf '[%s]\n' ./march-[0123456789][0123456789].txt|.
Only \texttt{./march-12.txt} is selected. This is a read-only
preview and creates nothing to clean up.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Reach below the working directory}
Write one shell glob for the single-digit March receipt inside
\texttt{archive}, then use \cmd|find| from the lab root to list
all the single-digit March receipts without selecting the hidden one.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
Preview \cmd|./archive/march-[123456789].txt| with \cmd|printf|
for one match. Then use \cmd|find . -type f -name 'march-[123456789].txt' -print|
for three matches: 1, 2, and 4. No files change.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Distinguish a literal argument from a result}
Use the original April preview in \cmd|sh| and in your normal
shell if it differs. Then quote the pattern in both. In each
case, decide whether a wildcard was expanded, a string was
passed as written, or the shell rejected the command.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
In normal POSIX \cmd|sh| both inputs print \texttt{[./april*.txt]};
the unquoted form tried to match a filename before leaving the
pattern unchanged. Default Zsh normally rejects the unquoted
form. The quoted form passes one literal argument in either
shell. Exit any extra practice shell; this test makes no files.
\end{solution}

Check \cmd|pwd| against the recorded lab path and stop if it
doesn't match. The named removal with \cmd|-i| confirms each
file; do not replace the list with a glob. If the directories
are not empty, \cmd|rmdir| will refuse; inspect the leftovers.

\begin{terminal}
rm -i march-1.txt march-2.txt march-12.txt 'march coffee.txt'
rm -i feb-1.txt march-1.pdf .march-3.txt archive/march-4.txt
rm -i march-summary.txt
rmdir archive
cd ..
rmdir "$lab"
\end{terminal}

\begin{takeaways}
\item An unquoted pathname glob is processed by the shell before the utility runs.
\item The slash divides components, and a leading dot normally needs an explicit match.
\item Preview before acting; an unchanged pattern is not evidence of a match.
\end{takeaways}

